        </style><script src="https://blog.bhanzu.com/_next/static/chunks/a6dad97d9634a72d.js" noModule=""></script></head><body style="font-family:&#x27;Quicksand&#x27;, &#x27;Quicksand Fallback&#x27;, sans-serif"><div hidden=""><!--$--><!--/$--></div><noscript><iframe src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/ns.html?id=GTM-5TXWM3B" height="0" width="0" style="display:none;visibility:hidden"></iframe></noscript><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://bhanzu.com/math/terms/meter#article","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://bhanzu.com/math/terms/meter"},"url":"https://bhanzu.com/math/terms/meter","headline":"What is Meter — Definition, Conversion & Examples in Math","description":"The meter is the SI base unit of length, equal to about 3.28 feet. Conversion chart (mm, cm, m, km), three worked examples, common mistakes, and where the meter comes from.","datePublished":"2026-06-05T07:29:34Z","dateModified":"2026-06-05T07:29:34Z","inLanguage":"en","articleSection":"Math Terms","wordCount":1333,"author":[{"@type":"Person","@id":"https://bhanzu.com/authors/bhanzu-team#person","name":"Bhanzu Team","url":"https://bhanzu.com/authors/bhanzu-team","jobTitle":"Content Creator and Editor","description":"Bhanzu’s editorial team, known as Team Bhanzu, is made up of experienced educators, curriculum experts, content strategists, and fact-checkers dedicated to making math simple and engaging for learners worldwide. Every article and resource is carefully researched, thoughtfully structured, and rigorously reviewed to ensure accuracy, clarity, and real-world relevance.\r\n\r\nWe understand that building strong math foundations can raise questions for students and parents alike. That’s why Team Bhanzu focuses on delivering practical insights, concept-driven explanations, and trustworthy guidance-empowering learners to develop confidence, speed, and a lifelong love for mathematics."}],"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","@id":"https://bhanzu.com/#organization","name":"Bhanzu","url":"https://bhanzu.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://bhanzu.com/brand/bhanzu-logo-600x60.png","width":600,"height":60},"sameAs":["https://www.linkedin.com/company/bhanzu","https://twitter.com/bhanzu_official","https://www.instagram.com/bhanzu_/","https://www.youtube.com/@bhanzu"]}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://bhanzu.com/math/terms/meter#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a meter in math?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The SI base unit of length, equal to $100$ centimetres or about $3.28$ feet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is the meter defined?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As the length light travels in vacuum in $1/299{,}792{,}458$ of a second."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many centimetres are in $1$ meter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"$100$ centimetres."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many feet are in $1$ meter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Approximately $3.281$ feet, or exactly $\\dfrac{1}{0.3048}$ feet (since $1$ ft $= 0.3048$ m)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many meters are in $1$ kilometre?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"$1{,}000$ meters."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What's the difference between \"meter\" and \"metre\"?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Spelling only. American English uses \"meter\"; British and most other English uses \"metre.\" They refer to the same unit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who invented the meter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The French Academy of Sciences proposed it in $1791$ as a unit \"for all people, for all time.\" It became the international standard with the Metric Convention of $1875$."}}]}</script><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/katex@0.16.9/dist/katex.min.css"/><div class="page-root" data-template="Default (id=1)" data-site="bhanzu.com" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-height:100vh;font-family:&#x27;Quicksand&#x27;,&#x27;Quicksand Fallback&#x27;,sans-serif;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.8;padding-top:0"><div style="position:sticky;top:0;z-index:100"><style>
        /* Full-width white strip */
        .bhnav-outer {
          width: 100%;
          background: #fff;
          padding: 16px 0 16px;
          box-sizing: border-box;
          /* Belt-and-braces: the breakpoint below is tuned to today's link
             widths, so adding a nav item could reintroduce the overflow at a
             wider viewport. Containing it here means the worst case is a
             clipped nav, never a page that scrolls sideways. */
          overflow-x: clip;
        }
        /* Centred 1280px container */
        .bhnav-container {
          max-width: 1280px;
          margin: 0 auto;
          padding: 0 24px;
          box-sizing: border-box;
        }
        /* The peach rounded card */
        .bhnav {
          background: #FDEEE8;
          border-radius: 16px;
          padding: 16px 32px;
          display: flex;
          align-items: center;
          justify-content: space-between;
          gap: 12px;
          font-family: inherit;
          position: sticky;
          top: 16px;
          z-index: 500;
        }

        /* Logo */
        .bhnav__logo { display: flex; align-items: center; flex-shrink: 0; text-decoration: none; }
        .bhnav__logo img { width: 120px; height: 36px; display: block; object-fit: contain; }

        /* Desktop links */
        .bhnav__links {
          display: flex;
          align-items: center;
          gap: 0;
          flex: 1;
          /* Without min-width:0 a flex item refuses to shrink below its
             content, which is what pushed the row past the viewport. */
          min-width: 0;
          justify-content: center;
          height: 100%;
        }
        .bhnav__link-wrap {
          position: relative;
          padding: 0 16px;
        }
        .bhnav__link {
          display: block;
          font-size: 16px;
          font-weight: 700;
          color: #413930;
          text-decoration: none;
          white-space: nowrap;
          font-family: inherit;
          line-height: 1.4;
        }
        .bhnav__link:hover { color: #F15D22; }

        /* wavy orange underline for active item */
        .bhnav__selector {
          position: absolute;
          bottom: -22px;
          left: 16px;
          right: 16px;
          height: 6px;
          background-image: url('https://blog.bhanzu.com/assets/nav/nav_selector.svg');
          background-size: 100% 100%;
          background-repeat: no-repeat;
          pointer-events: none;
        }

        /* Programs dropdown */
        .bhnav__link-wrap { position: relative; }
        /*
          Bridge the gap between the trigger and the menu.

          The menu sits 12px below the link, and that gap was dead space: moving
          the cursor down crossed it, :hover was lost, and the menu closed before
          it could be reached. Worse, the parent is a real link, so the click
          that followed navigated to /course-details instead of the submenu item
          the reader was aiming at.

          A transparent ::after spanning the gap keeps :hover alive across it.
          It is only present while hovering, so it never blocks clicks on the
          nav items beside it.
        */
        .bhnav__link-wrap::after {
          content: '';
          position: absolute;
          top: 100%;
          left: 0;
          right: 0;
          height: 14px;
          display: none;
        }
        .bhnav__link-wrap:hover::after { display: block; }
        .bhnav__dropdown {
          position: absolute;
          top: calc(100% + 12px);
          left: 50%;
          transform: translateX(-50%);
          background: #fff;
          border-radius: 10px;
          box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);
          min-width: 160px;
          padding: 6px 0;
          display: none;
          z-index: 600;
          white-space: nowrap;
        }
        .bhnav__link-wrap:hover .bhnav__dropdown,
        .bhnav__dropdown:hover,
        /* Keyboard parity: the menu was unreachable by tab, since it only ever
           opened on hover. */
        .bhnav__link-wrap:focus-within .bhnav__dropdown { display: block; }
        .bhnav__dropdown a {
          display: block;
          padding: 9px 18px;
          font-size: 15px;
          font-weight: 600;
          color: #413930;
          text-decoration: none;
          font-family: inherit;
        }
        .bhnav__dropdown a:hover { color: #F15D22; background: #FFF5F2; }
        .bhnav__caret {
          display: inline-block;
          margin-left: 3px;
          font-size: 11px;
          vertical-align: middle;
          line-height: 1;
          color: inherit;
          transition: transform 0.2s;
        }
        .bhnav__link-wrap:hover .bhnav__caret,
        .bhnav__link-wrap:focus-within .bhnav__caret { transform: rotate(180deg); }

        /* CTA button — exact bhanzu.com values */
        .bhnav__phone {
          font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: #413930;
          text-decoration: none; margin-right: 16px; white-space: nowrap;
        }
        .bhnav__phone:hover { color: #F15D22; }
        a.bhnav__cta { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; text-decoration: none; }
        .bhnav__cta {
          background: #F15D22;
          color: #fff !important;
          padding: 8px 28px;
          border-radius: 40px;
          font-weight: 700;
          font-size: 14px;
          text-decoration: none;
          white-space: nowrap;
          flex-shrink: 0;
          font-family: inherit;
          transition: background 0.15s, box-shadow 0.15s;
          display: inline-block;
        }
        .bhnav__cta:hover {
          background: #d94f1a;
          box-shadow: 0 2px 10px rgba(241,93,34,0.3);
        }
        /* CTA rendered as <button> — reset native button chrome to match the <a> look */
        button.bhnav__cta, button.bhnav__mobile-cta {
          border: none;
          cursor: pointer;
          font-family: inherit;
        }

        /* Demo-form popup modal */
        .bhnav__modal-overlay {
          position: fixed;
          inset: 0;
          background: rgba(15,31,61,0.55);
          z-index: 9999;
          display: flex;
          align-items: center;
          justify-content: center;
          padding: 16px;
        }
        .bhnav__modal {
          position: relative;
          background: #fff;
          border-radius: 16px;
          width: 100%;
          max-width: 480px;
          height: min(640px, 90vh);
          box-shadow: 0 24px 64px rgba(0,0,0,0.25);
          overflow: hidden;
          display: flex;
          flex-direction: column;
        }
        .bhnav__modal-close {
          position: absolute;
          top: 10px;
          right: 10px;
          z-index: 2;
          width: 32px;
          height: 32px;
          border-radius: 50%;
          border: none;
          background: rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
          color: #0F1F3D;
          font-size: 18px;
          line-height: 1;
          cursor: pointer;
          display: flex;
          align-items: center;
          justify-content: center;
        }
        .bhnav__modal-close:hover { background: rgba(0,0,0,0.12); }
        .bhnav__modal iframe {
          width: 100%;
          height: 100%;
          border: 0;
          display: block;
        }

        /* Mobile hamburger */
        .bhnav__hamburger-input { display: none; }
        .bhnav__hamburger-label {
          display: none;
          flex-direction: column;
          gap: 5px;
          cursor: pointer;
          padding: 6px;
          flex-shrink: 0;
        }
        .bhnav__hamburger-label span {
          display: block;
          width: 22px;
          height: 2px;
          background: #413930;
          border-radius: 2px;
          transition: transform 0.2s, opacity 0.2s;
        }
        .bhnav__mobile-menu {
          display: none;
          flex-direction: column;
          background: #FDEEE8;
          border-radius: 0 0 16px 16px;
          padding: 8px 16px 20px;
          gap: 4px;
        }
        .bhnav__mobile-menu .bhnav__link {
          padding: 10px 12px;
          border-radius: 8px;
          font-size: 15px;
        }
        .bhnav__mobile-cta {
          display: block;
          margin-top: 12px;
          background: #F15D22;
          color: #fff;
          padding: 10px 28px;
          border-radius: 40px;
          font-weight: 700;
          font-size: 14px;
          text-decoration: none;
          text-align: center;
          font-family: inherit;
        }

        /* Switch to the hamburger at 1130px, not 880px.
           The desktop row needs ~1129px: logo 120 + links 723 + CTA 214 +
           24px gaps + 48px container padding = 1129. Hiding it only below
           880px left an ~248px band (881-1129px) where the desktop nav was
           still showing but wider than the viewport, so the whole PAGE
           scrolled sideways by up to 238px -- on every page using this nav,
           across iPad portrait and landscape.
           Measured on production rather than guessed; see the flex-wrap
           guard below for the case where the link set grows. */
        @media (max-width: 1130px) {
          .bhnav { padding: 12px 16px; }
          .bhnav__links { display: none; }
          .bhnav__cta   { display: none; }
          .bhnav__hamburger-label { display: flex; }
          /* input is first child of .bhnav-container, so ~ reaches .bhnav and .bhnav__mobile-menu */
          .bhnav__hamburger-input:checked ~ .bhnav__mobile-menu { display: flex; }
          .bhnav__hamburger-input:checked ~ .bhnav .bhnav__hamburger-label span:nth-child(1) { transform: translateY(7px) rotate(45deg); }
          .bhnav__hamburger-input:checked ~ .bhnav .bhnav__hamburger-label span:nth-child(2) { opacity: 0; }
          .bhnav__hamburger-input:checked ~ .bhnav .bhnav__hamburger-label span:nth-child(3) { transform: translateY(-7px) rotate(-45deg); }
        }
        @media (max-width: 480px) {
          .bhnav-container { padding: 0 12px; }
        }
      </style><div class="bhnav-outer"><div class="bhnav-container"><input type="checkbox" id="bhnav-toggle" class="bhnav__hamburger-input" aria-hidden="true"/><div class="bhnav"><a href="https://bhanzu.com" class="bhnav__logo" aria-label="Bhanzu Home"><img src="https://blog.bhanzu.com/assets/logos/bhanzu_logo_black_md.png" alt="Bhanzu" width="120" height="36" loading="eager"/></a><nav class="bhnav__links" aria-label="Main navigation"><div class="bhnav__link-wrap"><a href="https://bhanzu.com" class="bhnav__link">Home</a></div><div class="bhnav__link-wrap"><a href="https://bhanzu.com/course-details" class="bhnav__link">Programs<span class="bhnav__caret" aria-hidden="true">▾</span></a><div class="bhnav__dropdown"><a href="https://bhanzu.com/course-details">Math Program</a><a href="https://bhanzu.com/english-course">English Program</a></div></div><div class="bhnav__link-wrap"><a href="https://bhanzu.com/about-us" class="bhnav__link">About Us</a></div><div class="bhnav__link-wrap"><a href="https://bhanzu.com/blogs" class="bhnav__link">Blogs</a><span class="bhnav__selector" aria-hidden="true"></span></div><div class="bhnav__link-wrap"><a href="https://app.bhanzu.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="bhnav__link">Student Dashboard</a></div><div class="bhnav__link-wrap"><a href="https://bhanzu.com/bt/become-a-trainer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="bhnav__link">Become a Trainer</a></div></nav><button type="button" class="bhnav__cta" aria-haspopup="dialog">Book A Free Math Class</button><label for="bhnav-toggle" class="bhnav__hamburger-label" aria-label="Toggle menu"><span></span><span></span><span></span></label></div><nav class="bhnav__mobile-menu" aria-label="Mobile navigation"><a href="https://bhanzu.com" class="bhnav__link">Home</a><a href="https://bhanzu.com/course-details" class="bhnav__link">Math Program</a><a href="https://bhanzu.com/english-course" class="bhnav__link">English Program</a><a href="https://bhanzu.com/about-us" class="bhnav__link">About Us</a><a href="https://bhanzu.com/blogs" class="bhnav__link">Blogs</a><a href="https://app.bhanzu.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="bhnav__link">Student Dashboard</a><a href="https://bhanzu.com/bt/become-a-trainer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="bhnav__link">Become a Trainer</a><button type="button" class="bhnav__mobile-cta" aria-haspopup="dialog">Book A Free Math Class</button></nav></div></div></div><div style="flex:1"><div class="page-grid" style="max-width:1320px;margin:0 auto;padding:28px 40px 60px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:200px 1fr 280px;column-gap:48px;row-gap:0;align-items:start"><div class="page-article-header" style="grid-column:2;grid-row:1;padding-bottom:24px;border-bottom:1px solid #E9ECEF"><nav style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;margin-bottom:18px;font-size:12px;font-weight:500;flex-wrap:wrap"><a style="color:#6B7280;text-decoration:none" href="/">Home</a><span style="color:#DEE2E6">/</span><a style="color:#6B7280;text-decoration:none;text-transform:capitalize" href="/math">math</a><span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px"><span style="color:#DEE2E6">/</span><a style="color:#6B7280;text-decoration:none" href="/math/terms">Terms</a></span><span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px"><span style="color:#DEE2E6">/</span><span style="color:#4B5563;font-weight:600">Meter</span></span></nav><h1 style="font-family:&#x27;Quicksand&#x27;,&#x27;Quicksand Fallback&#x27;,sans-serif;font-size:clamp(24px,3vw,38px);font-weight:900;color:#0F1F3D;line-height:1.2;margin:0 0 14px">What is Meter — Definition, Conversion &amp; Examples in Math</h1><div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin-bottom:16px"><a href="/tag/math-terms" style="background:#FFF4EF;color:#F15D22;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 12px;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;border:1px solid rgba(241,93,34,0.2);text-decoration:none">#<!-- -->Math Terms</a></div><div style="padding:16px 20px;background:#F9FAFB;border-radius:8px;font-size:14.5px;color:#4B5563;line-height:1.78;border-left:3px solid #F15D22;margin:0 0 20px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#F15D22;margin-bottom:6px">TL;DR</div>A meter (symbol $\mathrm{m}$) is the SI base unit of length — the international standard for measuring distance — equal to $100$ centimetres, $1000$ millimetres, and approximately $3.281$ feet. This article gives the formal definition, the metric prefix ladder, conversion to imperial units, three worked examples, and the most common conversion mistakes.</div><div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap;font-size:13px;color:#6B7280"><div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#F15D22,#FFB347);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:800;font-size:10px;color:#fff;flex-shrink:0">BT</div><a href="/authors/bhanzu-team" style="color:#F15D22;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Bhanzu Team</a><span style="width:1px;height:14px;background:#DEE2E6;margin:0 6px;display:inline-block"></span><span>Last updated on <!-- -->June 5, 2026</span><span style="width:1px;height:14px;background:#DEE2E6;margin:0 6px;display:inline-block"></span><span>8<!-- --> min read</span></div></div><aside class="page-toc-aside" style="grid-column:1;grid-row:1 / span 2;position:sticky;top:120px;align-self:start;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0;z-index:1"><div><div class="page-toc-title" style="font-size:14px;font-weight:800;color:#0F1F3D;margin-bottom:14px;font-family:Montserrat, sans-serif">Table of Contents</div><ul style="list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0;padding:0;margin:0;max-height:calc(100vh - 160px);overflow-y:auto"><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">The Formal Definition</button></li><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">The Metric Length Ladder — Prefixes and Powers of 10</button></li><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">Meter to Imperial — The Most-Used Conversions</button></li><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">Three Worked Examples of Meter — Quick, Standard, Stretch</button></li><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">Where the Meter Appears — Beyond the Tape Measure</button></li><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">Tripping Points to Avoid In Meter</button></li><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">Conclusion</button></li><li><button style="display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;padding:7px 0 7px 12px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:#6B7280;border-left:2px solid #E9ECEF;background:none;cursor:pointer;line-height:1.5;transition:all 0.15s;outline:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none">Quick Self-Check — Three Conversions</button></li></ul></div><div style="margin-top:20px"><div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:14px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #E9ECEF;margin-bottom:0"><span style="font-size:11.5px;font-weight:700;color:#ADB5BD;letter-spacing:0.06em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-right:4px">Share</span><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbhanzu.com%2Fmath%2Fterms%2Fmeter&amp;title=What%20is%20Meter%20%E2%80%94%20Definition%2C%20Conversion%20%26%20Examples%20in%20Math" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="w-[34px] h-[34px] rounded-lg border flex items-center justify-center cursor-pointer transition-all duration-200" style="border:1px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;color:#6B7280" title="Share on LinkedIn"><svg width="15" height="15" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M20.447 20.452h-3.554v-5.569c0-1.328-.027-3.037-1.852-3.037-1.853 0-2.136 1.445-2.136 2.939v5.667H9.351V9h3.414v1.561h.046c.477-.9 1.637-1.85 3.37-1.85 3.601 0 4.267 2.37 4.267 5.455v6.286zM5.337 7.433a2.062 2.062 0 01-2.063-2.065 2.064 2.064 0 112.063 2.065zm1.782 13.019H3.555V9h3.564v11.452zM22.225 0H1.771C.792 0 0 .774 0 1.729v20.542C0 23.227.792 24 1.771 24h20.451C23.2 24 24 23.227 24 22.271V1.729C24 .774 23.2 0 22.222 0h.003z"></path></svg></a><a href="https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fbhanzu.com%2Fmath%2Fterms%2Fmeter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="w-[34px] h-[34px] rounded-lg border flex items-center justify-center cursor-pointer transition-all duration-200" style="border:1px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;color:#6B7280" title="Share on Facebook"><svg width="15" height="15" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M24 12.073c0-6.627-5.373-12-12-12s-12 5.373-12 12c0 5.99 4.388 10.954 10.125 11.854v-8.385H7.078v-3.47h3.047V9.43c0-3.007 1.792-4.669 4.533-4.669 1.312 0 2.686.235 2.686.235v2.953H15.83c-1.491 0-1.956.925-1.956 1.874v2.25h3.328l-.532 3.47h-2.796v8.385C19.612 23.027 24 18.062 24 12.073z"></path></svg></a><a href="https://api.whatsapp.com/send?text=What%20is%20Meter%20%E2%80%94%20Definition%2C%20Conversion%20%26%20Examples%20in%20Math%20https%3A%2F%2Fbhanzu.com%2Fmath%2Fterms%2Fmeter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="w-[34px] h-[34px] rounded-lg border flex items-center justify-center cursor-pointer transition-all duration-200" style="border:1px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;color:#6B7280" title="Share on WhatsApp"><svg width="15" height="15" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M17.472 14.382c-.297-.149-1.758-.867-2.03-.967-.273-.099-.471-.148-.67.15-.197.297-.767.966-.94 1.164-.173.199-.347.223-.644.075-.297-.15-1.255-.463-2.39-1.475-.883-.788-1.48-1.761-1.653-2.059-.173-.297-.018-.458.13-.606.134-.133.298-.347.446-.52.149-.174.198-.298.298-.497.099-.198.05-.371-.025-.52-.075-.149-.669-1.612-.916-2.207-.242-.579-.487-.5-.669-.51-.173-.008-.371-.01-.57-.01-.198 0-.52.074-.792.372-.272.297-1.04 1.016-1.04 2.479 0 1.462 1.065 2.875 1.213 3.074.149.198 2.096 3.2 5.077 4.487.709.306 1.262.489 1.694.625.712.227 1.36.195 1.871.118.571-.085 1.758-.719 2.006-1.413.248-.694.248-1.289.173-1.413-.074-.124-.272-.198-.57-.347m-5.421 7.403h-.004a9.87 9.87 0 01-5.031-1.378l-.361-.214-3.741.982.998-3.648-.235-.374a9.86 9.86 0 01-1.51-5.26c.001-5.45 4.436-9.884 9.888-9.884 2.64 0 5.122 1.03 6.988 2.898a9.825 9.825 0 012.893 6.994c-.003 5.45-4.437 9.884-9.885 9.884m8.413-18.297A11.815 11.815 0 0012.05 0C5.495 0 .16 5.335.157 11.892c0 2.096.547 4.142 1.588 5.945L.057 24l6.305-1.654a11.882 11.882 0 005.683 1.448h.005c6.554 0 11.89-5.335 11.893-11.893a11.821 11.821 0 00-3.48-8.413Z"></path></svg></a><button class="w-[34px] h-[34px] rounded-lg border flex items-center justify-center cursor-pointer transition-all duration-200" style="border:1px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;color:#6B7280;border-color:#E9ECEF" title="Copy link"><svg width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2"></rect><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 01-2-2V4a2 2 0 012-2h9a2 2 0 012 2v1"></path></svg></button></div></div><div style="margin-top:20px;padding-top:16px;border-top:1px solid #E9ECEF"><div style="font-size:12px;font-weight:700;color:#0F1F3D;margin-bottom:8px">Was this helpful?</div><div style="display:flex;gap:9px"><div style="display:flex;gap:9px"><button style="width:38px;height:38px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;transition:all 0.2s" title="Yes, helpful">👍</button><button style="width:38px;height:38px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;transition:all 0.2s" title="Not helpful">👎</button></div></div></div></aside><article class="page-article-body" style="grid-column:2;grid-row:2;min-width:0;padding-top:24px;overflow:hidden"><div class="bhanzu-prose"><p>A <strong>meter</strong> is the worldwide standard unit of length. From shoe size to running track to highway distance, the meter (or its multiples and submultiples) is how most of the world measures.</p><h2 id="the-formal-definition">The Formal Definition</h2><p>The <strong>meter</strong> (or <strong>metre</strong> in British spelling) is the SI base unit of length. Since $1983$, it has been formally defined as:</p><blockquote><p><em>the length of the path travelled by light in a vacuum during a time interval of $\dfrac{1}{299{,}792{,}458}$ of a second.</em></p></blockquote><p>In simpler terms: the meter is defined through the speed of light, which is a universal constant. This definition guarantees that one meter in Paris equals one meter in Mumbai equals one meter on Mars — the same physical length, anywhere.</p><p>Earlier definitions (1791) had the meter as one ten-millionth of the distance from the equator to the North Pole along the meridian through Paris. By 1889 it became the length of a platinum-iridium bar held in Sèvres, France. The 1983 redefinition tied it to a universal constant of nature so that the unit could not drift over time.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Quick reference.</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Symbol:</strong> m (meter / metre).</p></li><li><p><strong>SI base unit for:</strong> length.</p></li><li><p><strong>Equivalents:</strong> $1$ m $= 100$ cm $= 1000$ mm $= 0.001$ km.</p></li><li><p><strong>Imperial:</strong> $1$ m $\approx 3.281$ feet $\approx 39.37$ inches $\approx 1.094$ yards.</p></li><li><p><strong>Grade introduced:</strong> CCSS-M <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://4.MD" class="md-link">4.MD</a>.A.1 (units of measurement); NCERT Class 5 Chapter — Tenths and Hundredths / Class 6 Mensuration.</p></li></ul></blockquote><h2 id="the-metric-length-ladder-prefixes-and-powers-of-10">The Metric Length Ladder — Prefixes and Powers of 10</h2><p>Every metric length unit is a power of $10$ times the meter. The seven most-used prefixes around the meter:</p><div class="table-scroll-wrapper"><table style="min-width: 75px;" class="md-table"><thead><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Unit</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Symbol</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">In meters</p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Kilometer</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">km</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$1{,}000$ m $= 10^{3}$ m</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Hectometer</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">hm</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$100$ m $= 10^{2}$ m</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Decameter</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">dam</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$10$ m</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;"><strong>Meter</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;"><strong>m</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;"><strong>$1$ m</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Decimeter</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">dm</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$0.1$ m $= 10^{-1}$ m</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Centimeter</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">cm</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$0.01$ m $= 10^{-2}$ m</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Millimeter</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">mm</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$0.001$ m $= 10^{-3}$ m</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>To convert between units, move the decimal point — one place per "step" on the ladder. Going down the ladder (m $\to$ cm $\to$ mm) means dividing by $10$ each time; going up (m $\to$ km) means dividing by larger powers.</p><p>A useful rule of thumb: km $\to$ m $\to$ cm $\to$ mm — each arrow is a $\times 1000$ then $\times 100$ then $\times 10$ if you spell out the gaps with dam, hm, dm.</p><h2 id="meter-to-imperial-the-most-used-conversions">Meter to Imperial — The Most-Used Conversions</h2><div class="table-scroll-wrapper"><table style="min-width: 100px;" class="md-table"><thead><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Meter</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Feet</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Inches</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">Yards</p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$1$ m</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$3.281$ ft</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$39.37$ in</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$1.094$ yd</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$5$ m</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$16.40$ ft</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$196.85$ in</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$5.47$ yd</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$10$ m</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$32.81$ ft</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$393.70$ in</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$10.94$ yd</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$100$ m</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$328.08$ ft</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$3{,}937.01$ in</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$109.36$ yd</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$1$ km</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$3{,}280.84$ ft</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">—</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p style="text-align: center;">$1{,}093.61$ yd</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A mile is $1{,}609.34$ m. A marathon ($42.195$ km) is exactly $26.2$ miles. A foot is exactly $0.3048$ m (this is a definition, not an approximation — adopted in $1959$ by both the US and UK).</p><h2 id="three-worked-examples-of-meter-quick-standard-stretch">Three Worked Examples of Meter — Quick, Standard, Stretch</h2><p><strong>Quick.</strong> Convert $3.5$ m to centimetres.</p><p>$1$ m $= 100$ cm, so $3.5$ m $= 3.5 \times 100 = 350$ cm.</p><p><strong>Final answer:</strong> $350$ cm.</p><p><strong>Standard (Wrong Path First — Where Conversions Slip).</strong> A field is $250$ m long. Express this in kilometres.</p><p><em>The wrong path.</em> A student multiplies: $250 \times 1000 = 250{,}000$ km.</p><p>The flaw: km is the <em>larger</em> unit. Going from a smaller unit (m) to a larger unit (km), you <em>divide</em>, not multiply. Multiplying by $1000$ gives a number that's a million times too big.</p><p><em>The rescue.</em> $1$ km $= 1000$ m, so $1$ m $= \dfrac{1}{1000}$ km. Therefore:</p><p>$$250 \text{ m} = \frac{250}{1000} \text{ km} = 0.25 \text{ km}.$$</p><p><strong>Final answer:</strong> $0.25$ km.</p><p>The lesson — <strong>going from a smaller unit to a larger one, divide; going from a larger to a smaller, multiply.</strong> The direction of conversion decides the operation.</p><p><strong>Stretch.</strong> A swimming pool is $50$ m long and $25$ m wide. Express the perimeter in (a) meters, (b) feet, and (c) yards. Round to two decimal places.</p><p>(a) Perimeter $= 2(l + b) = 2(50 + 25) = 150$ m.</p><p>(b) $1$ m $\approx 3.281$ ft, so $150 \times 3.281 \approx 492.15$ ft.</p><p>(c) $1$ m $\approx 1.094$ yd, so $150 \times 1.094 \approx 164.10$ yd.</p><p><strong>Final answer:</strong> (a) $150$ m, (b) $\approx 492.15$ ft, (c) $\approx 164.10$ yd.</p><p>This is the version of meter conversion problem that shows up in NCERT Class 6 mensuration and in any U.S. textbook covering imperial-metric conversion under CCSS-M <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://4.MD" class="md-link">4.MD</a>.</p><h2 id="where-the-meter-appears-beyond-the-tape-measure">Where the Meter Appears — Beyond the Tape Measure</h2><p>The meter is used everywhere length matters:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Sports.</strong> Olympic events are run in $100$-m, $200$-m, $400$-m, and $1500$-m distances. A track lane is exactly $1.22$ m wide. A football (soccer) field is $100$–$110$ m long.</p></li><li><p><strong>Construction.</strong> Floor plans, beam lengths, room dimensions — almost every country except the U.S. and Liberia uses metres directly. (U.S. construction still uses feet and inches.)</p></li><li><p><strong>Scientific work.</strong> Wavelengths of light (hundreds of nanometres), DNA strands (about $2$ nm wide), distances between Earth and the Sun ($1.496 \times 10^{11}$ m) — every length-based measurement in modern science is rooted in the meter.</p></li><li><p><strong>Cartography.</strong> A topographic map's contour lines are spaced in metres of elevation.</p></li><li><p><strong>GPS.</strong> A typical civilian GPS receiver locates you to within $5$–$10$ meters globally.</p></li></ul><p>The meter was created during the French Revolution by a commission of scientists including <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Laplace/" class="md-link">Pierre-Simon Laplace (1749–1827, France)</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lagrange/" class="md-link">Joseph-Louis Lagrange (1736–1813, Italy/France)</a> — the goal was a unit "for all people, for all time" rooted in nature rather than in the size of one king's foot. The metric system spread globally over the next $200$ years; by $2026$, every country except the United States, Liberia, and Myanmar uses it as the official measurement system.</p><h2 id="tripping-points-to-avoid-in-meter">Tripping Points to Avoid In Meter</h2><h3 id="mistake-1-multiplying-when-you-should-divide-or-vice-versa"><strong>Mistake 1: Multiplying when you should divide (or vice versa)</strong></h3><p><strong>Where it slips in:</strong> Converting metres to kilometres by multiplying.</p><p><strong>Don't do this:</strong> Use the same operation regardless of direction.</p><p><strong>The correct way:</strong> Smaller unit $\to$ larger unit: divide. Larger unit $\to$ smaller unit: multiply. $250$ m $= 0.25$ km, not $250{,}000$ km.</p><h3 id="mistake-2-mixing-metric-and-imperial-units-in-one-calculation"><strong>Mistake 2: Mixing metric and imperial units in one calculation</strong></h3><p><strong>Where it slips in:</strong> Adding $3$ m and $2$ ft directly.</p><p><strong>Don't do this:</strong> Treat metric and imperial units as interchangeable.</p><p><strong>The correct way:</strong> Convert to a single system first. $2$ ft $\approx 0.61$ m, so $3 + 0.61 = 3.61$ m. Or convert $3$ m to feet first.</p><h3 id="mistake-3-confusing-meter-length-with-meter-a-measuring-device"><strong>Mistake 3: Confusing meter (length) with meter (a measuring device)</strong></h3><p><strong>Where it slips in:</strong> "I bought a flow meter — is it $1$ m long?"</p><p><strong>Don't do this:</strong> Confuse the unit with the instrument.</p><p><strong>The correct way:</strong> The <em>meter</em> (unit) is a length. A <em>meter</em> (device — gas meter, parking meter, ammeter) is an instrument that measures something. The two share a name but mean different things.</p><p><strong>A real-world version of the mistake.</strong> The <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-climate-orbiter/" class="md-link">Mars Climate Orbiter</a> was lost in $1999$ because two engineering teams worked in different unit systems — one in pound-seconds (imperial), the other in newton-seconds (SI). The unit mismatch was never caught. The lesson is the same as the lesson on the bathroom scale: numbers without consistent units are dangerous.</p><h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2><ul><li><p>The <strong>meter</strong> (m) is the SI base unit of length.</p></li><li><p>It equals $100$ cm, $1000$ mm, and $0.001$ km — every metric length unit is a power of $10$ from the meter.</p></li><li><p>$1$ m $\approx 3.281$ feet; $1$ ft $= 0.3048$ m exactly.</p></li><li><p>Defined since $1983$ via the speed of light: the distance light travels in $1/299{,}792{,}458$ of a second.</p></li><li><p>Multiply when converting to a smaller unit, divide when converting to a larger one — the most common mistake reverses these.</p></li></ul><h2 id="quick-self-check-three-conversions">Quick Self-Check — Three Conversions</h2><ol><li><p>Convert $4.5$ km to meters.</p></li><li><p>Convert $750$ cm to meters.</p></li><li><p>Convert $10$ m to feet (use $1$ m $\approx 3.281$ ft).</p></li></ol><p>If problem 2 gave $75{,}000$ m, return to Mistake 1 above — you multiplied when you should have divided.</p><p>Want a live Bhanzu trainer to walk your child through unit conversion and metric system fluency? <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://bhanzu.com/book-demo-lp?utm_source=blog&amp;utm_medium=article&amp;utm_campaign=what-is-meter#book-demo" class="md-link">Book a free demo class</a> — online globally.</p></div><div style="margin:32px 0;border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden;border:1px solid #E9ECEF"><a href="https://bhanzu.com/embeddable-demo-form?utm_source=article-image-cta" style="display:block"><img src="https://static.bhanzu.com/blog-images/geometry-types-of-quadrilaterals/1784800838142-c8e01b21.webp" alt="Book a Free Demo" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block"/></a></div><div style="margin-top:24px;border:1px solid #E9ECEF;border-radius:14px;padding:18px 22px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap"><div><h4 style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:#374151;margin-bottom:2px">Was this article helpful?</h4><p style="font-size:12px;color:#ADB5BD">Your feedback helps us write better content</p></div><div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px"><button style="width:38px;height:38px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;transition:all 0.2s" title="Yes, helpful">👍</button><button style="width:38px;height:38px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid #E9ECEF;background:#fff;cursor:pointer;font-size:17px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;transition:all 0.2s" title="Not helpful">👎</button></div></div><div style="margin-top:36px"><h2 style="font-size:20px;font-weight:800;color:#0F1F3D;margin-bottom:16px">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="bhanzu-prose"><details class="md-accordion"><summary class="md-accordion-summary">What is a meter in math?</summary><div class="md-accordion-body">The SI base unit of length, equal to $100$ centimetres or about $3.28$ feet.</div></details><details class="md-accordion"><summary class="md-accordion-summary">How is the meter defined?</summary><div class="md-accordion-body">As the length light travels in vacuum in $1/299{,}792{,}458$ of a second.</div></details><details class="md-accordion"><summary class="md-accordion-summary">How many centimetres are in $1$ meter?</summary><div class="md-accordion-body">$100$ centimetres.</div></details><details class="md-accordion"><summary class="md-accordion-summary">How many feet are in $1$ meter?</summary><div class="md-accordion-body">Approximately $3.281$ feet, or exactly $\dfrac{1}{0.3048}$ feet (since $1$ ft $= 0.3048$ m).</div></details><details class="md-accordion"><summary class="md-accordion-summary">How many meters are in $1$ kilometre?</summary><div class="md-accordion-body">$1{,}000$ meters.</div></details><details class="md-accordion"><summary class="md-accordion-summary">What&#x27;s the difference between &quot;meter&quot; and &quot;metre&quot;?</summary><div class="md-accordion-body">Spelling only. American English uses &quot;meter&quot;; British and most other English uses &quot;metre.&quot; They refer to the same unit.</div></details><details class="md-accordion"><summary class="md-accordion-summary">Who invented the meter?</summary><div class="md-accordion-body">The French Academy of Sciences proposed it in $1791$ as a unit &quot;for all people, for all time.&quot; It became the international standard with the Metric Convention of $1875$.</div></details></div></div><div style="margin-top:36px;border:1px solid #E9ECEF;border-radius:16px;padding:20px;background:#FAFAFA"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#F15D22;margin-bottom:14px">✍️ Written By</div><div style="display:flex;gap:14px;align-items:flex-start"><div style="width:52px;height:52px;border-radius:50%;flex-shrink:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:15px;font-weight:800;color:#fff;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#F15D22,#FFB347);border:2px solid #fff">BT</div><div style="min-width:0;flex:1"><a href="/authors/bhanzu-team" style="font-family:&#x27;Quicksand&#x27;,&#x27;Quicksand Fallback&#x27;,sans-serif;font-size:15px;font-weight:800;color:#F15D22;margin-bottom:2px;text-decoration:none;display:block">Bhanzu Team</a><div style="font-size:12px;color:#6B7280;margin-bottom:6px">Content Creator and Editor</div><div style="font-size:13px;color:#4B5563;line-height:1.65">Bhanzu’s editorial team, known as Team Bhanzu, is made up of experienced educators, curriculum experts, content strategists, and fact-checkers dedicated to making math simple and engaging for learners worldwide. Every article and resource is carefully researched, thoughtfully structured, and rigorously reviewed to ensure accuracy, clarity, and real-world relevance.

We understand that building strong math foundations can raise questions for students and parents alike. That’s why Team Bhanzu focuses on delivering practical insights, concept-driven explanations, and trustworthy guidance-empowering learners to develop confidence, speed, and a lifelong love for mathematics.</div></div></div></div><div style="margin-top:20px;border:1px solid #E9ECEF;border-radius:14px;padding:16px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#ADB5BD;margin-bottom:12px">Related Articles</div><div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px"><a style="text-decoration:none;display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start" href="/math/geometry/radius"><div style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#0F1F3D;line-height:1.4;flex:1">Radius of a Circle: Definition, Formula, Examples</div></a><a style="text-decoration:none;display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start" href="/math/formula/cp"><div style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#0F1F3D;line-height:1.4;flex:1">CP Formula — How to Find Cost Price, Examples</div></a><a style="text-decoration:none;display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start" href="/math/formula/isosceles-triangles-formula"><div style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#0F1F3D;line-height:1.4;flex:1">Isosceles Triangle Formula — Area, Perimeter, Height</div></a><a style="text-decoration:none;display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start" href="/math/terms/chord"><div style="font-size:12.5px;font-weight:700;color:#0F1F3D;line-height:1.4;flex:1">What Is a Chord? Definition, Properties &amp; Examples</div></a></div></div></article><aside class="page-sidebar-aside" style="grid-column:3;grid-row:1 / span 2;position:sticky;top:120px;align-self:start;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px;z-index:1"><div style="background:#fff;border:1.5px solid #E9ECEF;border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden"><div style="background:linear-gradient(145deg,#EEF2FF,#F0F4FF);padding:20px 18px 14px;text-align:center"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6366F1;margin-bottom:8px">Math · UKG to Grade 9</div><h3 style="font-family:Quicksand,sans-serif;font-size:15px;font-weight:800;color:#0F1F3D;line-height:1.35;margin-bottom:6px">Looking for a personal math tutor for your child?</h3><div style="font-size:12px;color:#6B7280;line-height:1.6">Live 1:6 classes with India&#x27;s fastest human calculator</div><div style="width:100%;height:90px;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#C7D2FE,#A5B4FC);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:40px;margin:12px 0 0">👨‍🏫👧</div></div><div style="padding:16px 18px"><button style="display:block;width:100%;background:#6366F1;color:#fff;padding:11px 14px;border-radius:8px;font-weight:700;font-size:13.5px;text-align:center;font-family:Quicksand,sans-serif;border:none;cursor:pointer">Book FREE Demo Lesson</button><div style="font-size:10.5px;color:#ADB5BD;text-align:center;margin-top:8px">✓ Free · ✓ Live class · ✓ No card needed</div></div></div><div style="border:1px solid #E9ECEF;border-radius:14px;padding:16px"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#ADB5BD;margin-bottom:10px">Topics</div><div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px"><a href="/tag/math-terms" style="background:#FFF4EF;color:#F15D22;border-radius:20px;padding:3px 10px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;border:1px solid rgba(241,93,34,0.2);text-decoration:none">#<!-- -->Math Terms</a></div></div></aside></div></div><style>
